-- gate: reject
-- defect: an unqualified name bound by TWO `exposing (..)` imports
-- rust: reject [E1012] ambiguous unqualified name, reported at the use site
--
-- Found 2026-08-11 by the generated corpus's `stdlib_import` stratum (family S).
-- `Sky.Core.String` and `Sky.Core.List` both export `length`, both are imported
-- `exposing (..)`, and the reference below is bare. This program used to compile
-- clean and resolve `length` to whichever module was imported LAST — so swapping
-- the two import lines silently changed what the program computed, with no
-- diagnostic either way. `corpus/repro/ambiguous-exposing-all/` pins the
-- value-level half of the same defect: two hand-written projects, byte-identical
-- apart from import order, one printing ALPHA and one printing BETA.
--
-- Reordering imports is something a formatter, a merge, or an added import does
-- routinely, so "last import wins" turns a difference that must not matter into
-- a different program. That is the #164 family — whole-program name resolution
-- as a source of silent behaviour change — and Elm rejects it.
--
-- WHY IT SURVIVED. The naive rule ("a name bound by two imports is an error")
-- REJECTS WORKING PROGRAMS: `import Sky.Core.Prelude exposing (..)` together
-- with `import Sky.Core.Math exposing (..)` already double-binds `abs` / `min` /
-- `max` / `sqrt`, and real examples do exactly that. So the fix was blocked on a
-- precedence decision, not on the detection.
--
-- FIX. `hir::resolve::BindLayer` — a precedence lattice over unqualified
-- bindings (ambient < open < explicit < local). A name bound in several layers
-- resolves to the highest, deterministically and independently of import order;
-- only a tie INSIDE the winning layer is ambiguous. `Sky.Core.Prelude` is
-- ambient (autoloaded — `sky init` emits the import unconditionally), so an
-- explicit import shadows it silently and the Prelude/Math overlap keeps
-- compiling. The error is raised at the USE SITE, never at the import, because
-- importing two modules that both expose a name you never reference is legal and
-- extremely common — every Sky.Live page imports `Std.Html exposing (..)`
-- alongside `Std.Html.Attributes exposing (..)`.
--
-- The ACCEPTED twin is `ambiguous_unqualified_name_twin` in
-- `hir/src/lib.rs`'s test module (plus the `String.length`-qualified form), so
-- this rejection cannot be satisfied by a compiler that rejects everything.
module Main exposing (main)

import Sky.Core.Prelude exposing (..)
import Std.Log exposing (println)
import Sky.Core.String exposing (..)
import Sky.Core.List exposing (..)

main =
    println (String.fromInt (length "abcd"))
